🧠 Caching

Caching is one of the most useful concepts in functools.

Suppose a function takes a long time:

def expensive_function(n):
    # expensive calculation
    return result

If we call:

expensive_function(10)
expensive_function(10)
expensive_function(10)

without caching, the calculation may be repeated.

With caching:

expensive_function(10)
       ↓
Calculate
       ↓
Store result
       ↓
Cache


expensive_function(10)
       ↓
Cached result


expensive_function(10)
       ↓
Cached result

This can improve performance for suitable functions.

🧠 LRU Cache

LRU means:

Least Recently Used

When a cache has a maximum size and becomes full, the least recently used cached entries can be removed to make room for newer entries.

Example:

from functools import lru_cache


@lru_cache(maxsize=128)
def calculate(n):
    return n * n
